[[:db/retract 17592186152481 :question/answer-fields 17592186149460] [:db/retract 17592186152473 :question/answer-fields 17592186149387] [:db/retract 17592186152473 :question/answer-fields 17592186149383] [:db/retract 17592186152482 :question/answer-fields 17592186149466] {:db/id 17592186152058 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-23919475-35c0-5151-a80d-eb2f21c5c2a9" "question-0b565593-fa14-5d8e-a413-f5f33c1ea21c" "question-f913a8a8-9a07-5f52-8a63-48e2094f05b2" "question-0ae69b79-17cb-5f18-82ec-b83f30862ca1" 17592186152482 "question-5f92504f-2a88-577a-b7ee-007131f73594"] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Congruences" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151834 :question/problem "If $x + 8 \\equiv 11\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,15),$ find an equivalent statement where $x$ is isolated." :question/worked-solution "The addition property of congruences states that \n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+c\\equiv b+c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a-c\\equiv b-c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe use the subtraction property to subtract $8$ from both sides of the given congruence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+8 & ≡11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+8-8 & ≡11-8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152059 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1f9b7d3e-524b-551d-8a94-9d846396890c" "question-c78b30a9-b100-5475-8a11-aa23c310f873" "question-abd575a7-0f26-5465-b126-ff96772a8759" "question-3911df9a-6841-59e6-b534-6e8a3aa2e9ba" "question-6d54f83a-3712-59c6-b238-0e2bb4334321"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151836 :question/problem "Which of the following numbers is congruent to $0$ modulo $18?$\n\n- $-45$\n\n- $42$\n\n- $-54$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that  \n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in\\mathbb Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $18$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $18.$\n\nSo, since $18\\,|\\,(-54),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n-54 \\equiv (-3) \\cdot {\\color{blue}18} \\equiv (-3)\\cdot 0\\equiv 0 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}18}).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $18.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152060 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bc99e19c-49e9-51e2-8ead-f8afe5651f31" "question-77cbdd5d-15bd-5f9e-950f-9243376a199b" "question-d19c623d-9750-5d15-989b-ed8e917e3481"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151839 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $x \\equiv 23\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)?$ \n\n- $x \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n\n- $x \\equiv 3 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n\n- $x \\equiv 5 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$" :question/worked-solution "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  ${\\color{blue}6} \\cdot (-3) \\equiv 0 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}6})$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡23+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x & ≡23+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡23+6\\cdot (-3)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡23-18\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152061 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-700ec10d-3e3a-58fc-aadf-2fee69932797" "question-50be2be7-4190-59bf-9880-ce7373f52df0" "question-f453d5f9-efe9-5d81-aae2-3ab414bd5f85" "question-83693413-601d-53f0-b546-e5ed9a2ee614"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151863 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1896, and April 6th, 1896. \n\n- There were $2$ months with $31$ days (January and March).\n\n- There were $0$ months with $30$ days.\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February) since it was a leap year.\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and April 6th is given by\n\n$$\nd \n= \\overbrace{31(2) + 30(0) + 29(1)}^{\\textrm{days to April 1st}} + 5.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31 \\equiv 3, \\qquad 30\\equiv 2, \\qquad 29\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =2(31)+0(30)+1(29)+5 \\\\  & ≡2(3)+0(2)+1(1)+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡6+0+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $5$ days after January 1st, 1896, namely Monday." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-23919475-35c0-5151-a80d-eb2f21c5c2a9" :question/id #uuid "23919475-35c0-5151-a80d-eb2f21c5c2a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79667" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $x+5≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)?$" :question/worked-solution "The addition property of congruences states that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a+c≡b+c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-c≡b-c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe use the subtraction property to subtract $5$ from both sides of the given congruence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & ≡11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+5-5 & ≡11-5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-921948e6-4b81-53f0-b19f-76fb55d28b4e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-921948e6-4b81-53f0-b19f-76fb55d28b4e" :answer-field/id #uuid "921948e6-4b81-53f0-b19f-76fb55d28b4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f7b9b840-81ea-525f-848f-6a73e584494c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-42ff318e-e994-50d1-bf94-2696290ab970" "answer-2c4f9473-abce-522a-8bca-48ad3793f293" "answer-c0c81f46-8655-5936-ad36-f43d525b2632" "answer-81471d19-b8e8-5c4f-adc4-d7f45564b1e7" "answer-f7b9b840-81ea-525f-848f-6a73e584494c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42ff318e-e994-50d1-bf94-2696290ab970" :answer/id #uuid "42ff318e-e994-50d1-bf94-2696290ab970" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c4f9473-abce-522a-8bca-48ad3793f293" :answer/id #uuid "2c4f9473-abce-522a-8bca-48ad3793f293" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0c81f46-8655-5936-ad36-f43d525b2632" :answer/id #uuid "c0c81f46-8655-5936-ad36-f43d525b2632" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81471d19-b8e8-5c4f-adc4-d7f45564b1e7" :answer/id #uuid "81471d19-b8e8-5c4f-adc4-d7f45564b1e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7b9b840-81ea-525f-848f-6a73e584494c" :answer/id #uuid "f7b9b840-81ea-525f-848f-6a73e584494c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1f9b7d3e-524b-551d-8a94-9d846396890c" :question/id #uuid "1f9b7d3e-524b-551d-8a94-9d846396890c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132085" :question/problem "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $15?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $15$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $15.$\n\nTherefore, since $15\\,|\\,60,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n60≡4\\cdot 15≡4\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $15.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8554aa24-0dd3-5f08-91e8-2982a7c41834"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8554aa24-0dd3-5f08-91e8-2982a7c41834" :answer-field/id #uuid "8554aa24-0dd3-5f08-91e8-2982a7c41834" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3d250d98-53be-5872-b250-2cfdd5b52870" :answer-field/choices ["answer-26fb6187-2ddc-5cb5-90e4-735971aabc57" "answer-13926285-aa6b-5092-9d57-fa8e5980190e" "answer-3d250d98-53be-5872-b250-2cfdd5b52870" "answer-410f4b23-b18f-514c-8538-727b7e177f64" "answer-defce10e-4a66-50b5-aab9-31620eabe1ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26fb6187-2ddc-5cb5-90e4-735971aabc57" :answer/id #uuid "26fb6187-2ddc-5cb5-90e4-735971aabc57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13926285-aa6b-5092-9d57-fa8e5980190e" :answer/id #uuid "13926285-aa6b-5092-9d57-fa8e5980190e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "55" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d250d98-53be-5872-b250-2cfdd5b52870" :answer/id #uuid "3d250d98-53be-5872-b250-2cfdd5b52870" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-410f4b23-b18f-514c-8538-727b7e177f64" :answer/id #uuid "410f4b23-b18f-514c-8538-727b7e177f64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-defce10e-4a66-50b5-aab9-31620eabe1ae" :answer/id #uuid "defce10e-4a66-50b5-aab9-31620eabe1ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "70" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c78b30a9-b100-5475-8a11-aa23c310f873" :question/id #uuid "c78b30a9-b100-5475-8a11-aa23c310f873" :question/math-academy-id "q-79693" :question/problem "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $4$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $4.$\n\nTherefore, since $4\\,|\\,52,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n52≡13\\cdot 4≡13\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3da627ac-6ece-5e61-b584-128e542daafa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3da627ac-6ece-5e61-b584-128e542daafa" :answer-field/id #uuid "3da627ac-6ece-5e61-b584-128e542daafa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a71abade-3bbd-59bd-ae4b-682c7da3ce96" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d7407095-7271-5a85-b624-8d937c6a68b5" "answer-4d3842dd-fbdf-59ec-a53a-44baad664fb8" "answer-a71abade-3bbd-59bd-ae4b-682c7da3ce96" "answer-89490175-146d-532d-8f68-493b93815c29" "answer-b6542486-eb68-5ede-b9c6-a6800c1b48be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7407095-7271-5a85-b624-8d937c6a68b5" :answer/id #uuid "d7407095-7271-5a85-b624-8d937c6a68b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "26" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d3842dd-fbdf-59ec-a53a-44baad664fb8" :answer/id #uuid "4d3842dd-fbdf-59ec-a53a-44baad664fb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a71abade-3bbd-59bd-ae4b-682c7da3ce96" :answer/id #uuid "a71abade-3bbd-59bd-ae4b-682c7da3ce96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89490175-146d-532d-8f68-493b93815c29" :answer/id #uuid "89490175-146d-532d-8f68-493b93815c29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "62" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6542486-eb68-5ede-b9c6-a6800c1b48be" :answer/id #uuid "b6542486-eb68-5ede-b9c6-a6800c1b48be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "70" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-abd575a7-0f26-5465-b126-ff96772a8759" :question/id #uuid "abd575a7-0f26-5465-b126-ff96772a8759" :question/math-academy-id "q-132087" :question/problem "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $16?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $16$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $16.$ \n\nTherefore, since $16\\,|\\,64,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n64≡4\\cdot 16≡4\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $16.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae42fde0-2dea-5dfb-9792-c0acd061d911"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae42fde0-2dea-5dfb-9792-c0acd061d911" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae42fde0-2dea-5dfb-9792-c0acd061d911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e576421c-d593-5aa6-9e50-abdf4fe2585e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef70bf99-fcf0-55ec-928b-12c6c2ca010b" "answer-53963393-a2e3-5e41-8999-eeb1f37a839f" "answer-e576421c-d593-5aa6-9e50-abdf4fe2585e" "answer-3951433c-8ef2-53fb-9fa1-a20d8c2e401f" "answer-9d3dfac6-2843-5cc0-80c8-368a37714dc9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef70bf99-fcf0-55ec-928b-12c6c2ca010b" :answer/id #uuid "ef70bf99-fcf0-55ec-928b-12c6c2ca010b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "46" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53963393-a2e3-5e41-8999-eeb1f37a839f" :answer/id #uuid "53963393-a2e3-5e41-8999-eeb1f37a839f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e576421c-d593-5aa6-9e50-abdf4fe2585e" :answer/id #uuid "e576421c-d593-5aa6-9e50-abdf4fe2585e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3951433c-8ef2-53fb-9fa1-a20d8c2e401f" :answer/id #uuid "3951433c-8ef2-53fb-9fa1-a20d8c2e401f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "68" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d3dfac6-2843-5cc0-80c8-368a37714dc9" :answer/id #uuid "9d3dfac6-2843-5cc0-80c8-368a37714dc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3911df9a-6841-59e6-b534-6e8a3aa2e9ba" :question/id #uuid "3911df9a-6841-59e6-b534-6e8a3aa2e9ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-79694" :question/problem "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $17?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $17$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $17.$\n\nTherefore, since $17\\,|\\,(-51),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n-51≡(-3)\\cdot 17≡(-3)\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,17).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $17.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b0cbc6a9-d6ad-5825-87ad-9f60b699d5e0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b0cbc6a9-d6ad-5825-87ad-9f60b699d5e0" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0cbc6a9-d6ad-5825-87ad-9f60b699d5e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e841eb27-2ba6-5d57-96d0-00c04250bf76" :answer-field/choices ["answer-572f2823-d978-5e43-bc01-bf4131aaf0e1" "answer-35b43e3a-2682-543e-af75-81e03ac3fd70" "answer-e841eb27-2ba6-5d57-96d0-00c04250bf76" "answer-3d760f0e-a05f-5ea8-8540-e476ce0d1fa4" "answer-643fb83e-73bf-55aa-aa50-7d21a7839b8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-572f2823-d978-5e43-bc01-bf4131aaf0e1" :answer/id #uuid "572f2823-d978-5e43-bc01-bf4131aaf0e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35b43e3a-2682-543e-af75-81e03ac3fd70" :answer/id #uuid "35b43e3a-2682-543e-af75-81e03ac3fd70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e841eb27-2ba6-5d57-96d0-00c04250bf76" :answer/id #uuid "e841eb27-2ba6-5d57-96d0-00c04250bf76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-51" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d760f0e-a05f-5ea8-8540-e476ce0d1fa4" :answer/id #uuid "3d760f0e-a05f-5ea8-8540-e476ce0d1fa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-643fb83e-73bf-55aa-aa50-7d21a7839b8b" :answer/id #uuid "643fb83e-73bf-55aa-aa50-7d21a7839b8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6d54f83a-3712-59c6-b238-0e2bb4334321" :question/id #uuid "6d54f83a-3712-59c6-b238-0e2bb4334321" :question/math-academy-id "q-79692" :question/problem "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $14?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $14$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $14.$\n\nTherefore, since $14\\,|\\,(-84),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n-84≡(-6)\\cdot 14≡(-6)\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $14.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68cf371f-9717-5edb-92f6-5993a4fc7b4a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68cf371f-9717-5edb-92f6-5993a4fc7b4a" :answer-field/id #uuid "68cf371f-9717-5edb-92f6-5993a4fc7b4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0a659048-5bd4-5f99-9f41-4dcb9c78e3e6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1fa5f3a4-adf7-58c3-b075-de7d7b496c72" "answer-0a659048-5bd4-5f99-9f41-4dcb9c78e3e6" "answer-477d2d41-3b01-5f96-9359-1e22f47d5b8b" "answer-09245c92-930e-5f0d-9d1f-efcbe7d45bf3" "answer-511e3ece-b1ca-50d5-a0c8-f7b853acbdf7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fa5f3a4-adf7-58c3-b075-de7d7b496c72" :answer/id #uuid "1fa5f3a4-adf7-58c3-b075-de7d7b496c72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a659048-5bd4-5f99-9f41-4dcb9c78e3e6" :answer/id #uuid "0a659048-5bd4-5f99-9f41-4dcb9c78e3e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-84" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-477d2d41-3b01-5f96-9359-1e22f47d5b8b" :answer/id #uuid "477d2d41-3b01-5f96-9359-1e22f47d5b8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09245c92-930e-5f0d-9d1f-efcbe7d45bf3" :answer/id #uuid "09245c92-930e-5f0d-9d1f-efcbe7d45bf3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-511e3ece-b1ca-50d5-a0c8-f7b853acbdf7" :answer/id #uuid "511e3ece-b1ca-50d5-a0c8-f7b853acbdf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "44" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bc99e19c-49e9-51e2-8ead-f8afe5651f31" :question/id #uuid "bc99e19c-49e9-51e2-8ead-f8afe5651f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-132097" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)?$" :question/worked-solution "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  $7\\cdot 3≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡5+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ x & ≡5+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5+7\\cdot 3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5+21\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡26\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dcbe6109-bc46-5ab0-9f30-3701f2c81e8a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dcbe6109-bc46-5ab0-9f30-3701f2c81e8a" :answer-field/id #uuid "dcbe6109-bc46-5ab0-9f30-3701f2c81e8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e34b7e67-2e2a-5a2d-9934-09874839fbcf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c14a6194-233e-52ea-b81d-817aae45fcd9" "answer-226134f3-e697-5f75-9604-0da171ad901b" "answer-bea184a4-5a95-5a73-a77c-f8431f0b4e9b" "answer-e34b7e67-2e2a-5a2d-9934-09874839fbcf" "answer-e7ed3293-96fa-5701-98ca-7926f6b2af1a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c14a6194-233e-52ea-b81d-817aae45fcd9" :answer/id #uuid "c14a6194-233e-52ea-b81d-817aae45fcd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-226134f3-e697-5f75-9604-0da171ad901b" :answer/id #uuid "226134f3-e697-5f75-9604-0da171ad901b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡24\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bea184a4-5a95-5a73-a77c-f8431f0b4e9b" :answer/id #uuid "bea184a4-5a95-5a73-a77c-f8431f0b4e9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡25\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e34b7e67-2e2a-5a2d-9934-09874839fbcf" :answer/id #uuid "e34b7e67-2e2a-5a2d-9934-09874839fbcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7ed3293-96fa-5701-98ca-7926f6b2af1a" :answer/id #uuid "e7ed3293-96fa-5701-98ca-7926f6b2af1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡28\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-77cbdd5d-15bd-5f9e-950f-9243376a199b" :question/id #uuid "77cbdd5d-15bd-5f9e-950f-9243376a199b" :question/math-academy-id "q-79656" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $x≡35\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)?$" :question/worked-solution "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  $11\\cdot (-3)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡35+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡35+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡35+11\\cdot (-3)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡35-33\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-24d24caf-30f7-5551-a3d0-554479ace0e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-24d24caf-30f7-5551-a3d0-554479ace0e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "24d24caf-30f7-5551-a3d0-554479ace0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-65c87c87-940c-5fe9-85d0-d07bcbc79886" :answer-field/choices ["answer-65c87c87-940c-5fe9-85d0-d07bcbc79886" "answer-6130bea6-e153-597b-a976-04ba16161ccf" "answer-11771110-3806-54e3-a052-d7cb39b48fc0" "answer-9f6ff12a-77da-5cff-beb6-c7fb418f3147" "answer-74c83fb2-0003-50f0-85fe-daa92f7603a2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65c87c87-940c-5fe9-85d0-d07bcbc79886" :answer/id #uuid "65c87c87-940c-5fe9-85d0-d07bcbc79886" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6130bea6-e153-597b-a976-04ba16161ccf" :answer/id #uuid "6130bea6-e153-597b-a976-04ba16161ccf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11771110-3806-54e3-a052-d7cb39b48fc0" :answer/id #uuid "11771110-3806-54e3-a052-d7cb39b48fc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f6ff12a-77da-5cff-beb6-c7fb418f3147" :answer/id #uuid "9f6ff12a-77da-5cff-beb6-c7fb418f3147" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74c83fb2-0003-50f0-85fe-daa92f7603a2" :answer/id #uuid "74c83fb2-0003-50f0-85fe-daa92f7603a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d19c623d-9750-5d15-989b-ed8e917e3481" :question/id #uuid "d19c623d-9750-5d15-989b-ed8e917e3481" :question/math-academy-id "q-132093" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $x≡26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)?$" :question/worked-solution "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  $12\\cdot (-2)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡26+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ x & ≡26+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡26+12\\cdot (-2)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡26-24\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6519838-5e86-5791-bf74-fda374f69c7a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6519838-5e86-5791-bf74-fda374f69c7a" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6519838-5e86-5791-bf74-fda374f69c7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-85c90695-7b13-51e8-ae35-ae006e4c9ef2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85c90695-7b13-51e8-ae35-ae006e4c9ef2" "answer-b9a95d9f-7b79-5880-b604-8eec030fc7fe" "answer-a46f14e5-0e1b-50b0-be84-6fffb64d7571" "answer-ed626ba6-cb87-5f12-8f75-aa44a083d800" "answer-26354798-307b-5557-a789-f21a69500cd2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85c90695-7b13-51e8-ae35-ae006e4c9ef2" :answer/id #uuid "85c90695-7b13-51e8-ae35-ae006e4c9ef2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9a95d9f-7b79-5880-b604-8eec030fc7fe" :answer/id #uuid "b9a95d9f-7b79-5880-b604-8eec030fc7fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a46f14e5-0e1b-50b0-be84-6fffb64d7571" :answer/id #uuid "a46f14e5-0e1b-50b0-be84-6fffb64d7571" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed626ba6-cb87-5f12-8f75-aa44a083d800" :answer/id #uuid "ed626ba6-cb87-5f12-8f75-aa44a083d800" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26354798-307b-5557-a789-f21a69500cd2" :answer/id #uuid "26354798-307b-5557-a789-f21a69500cd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-700ec10d-3e3a-58fc-aadf-2fee69932797" :question/id #uuid "700ec10d-3e3a-58fc-aadf-2fee69932797" :question/math-academy-id "q-213291" :question/problem "A parodic holiday, International Talk Like a Pirate Day, was celebrated for the first time on September 19th, 1995. Given that January 1st, 1995, was a Sunday, use congruences to determine on which day of the week this holiday was celebrated for the first time.\n\n*Hint: The year 1995 was not a leap year since it is not divisible by $4.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1995, and September 1st, 1995. \n\n- There were $5$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July,  and  August).\n\n- There were $2$ months with $30$ days (April and  June).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and September 19th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to September 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(5)+30(2)+28(1)}}+18.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,18≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5(31)+2(30)+1(28)+18 \\\\  & ≡5(3)+2(2)+1(0)+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡15+4+0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1+4+0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $2$ days after January 1st, 1995, namely Tuesday." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44b86fd1-f11f-540e-b60d-259357d02f7d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44b86fd1-f11f-540e-b60d-259357d02f7d" :answer-field/id #uuid "44b86fd1-f11f-540e-b60d-259357d02f7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f70c49a-6289-5d97-a367-f807a502fdf0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cbd3be64-1f58-5b89-a253-861edb8875ac" "answer-74bba275-65d4-5278-9134-c9bd06a4c526" "answer-45e002a6-05ef-5373-af42-302884d63b31" "answer-6f70c49a-6289-5d97-a367-f807a502fdf0" "answer-a8c9b75b-8faf-584e-8656-ef50fe441a42"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbd3be64-1f58-5b89-a253-861edb8875ac" :answer/id #uuid "cbd3be64-1f58-5b89-a253-861edb8875ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Friday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74bba275-65d4-5278-9134-c9bd06a4c526" :answer/id #uuid "74bba275-65d4-5278-9134-c9bd06a4c526" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Monday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45e002a6-05ef-5373-af42-302884d63b31" :answer/id #uuid "45e002a6-05ef-5373-af42-302884d63b31" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Thursday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f70c49a-6289-5d97-a367-f807a502fdf0" :answer/id #uuid "6f70c49a-6289-5d97-a367-f807a502fdf0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Tuesday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8c9b75b-8faf-584e-8656-ef50fe441a42" :answer/id #uuid "a8c9b75b-8faf-584e-8656-ef50fe441a42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Wednesday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-50be2be7-4190-59bf-9880-ce7373f52df0" :question/id #uuid "50be2be7-4190-59bf-9880-ce7373f52df0" :question/math-academy-id "q-213211" :question/problem "Jacopo Peri's Euridice, modern opera's earliest known, fully surviving work, was first performed in Florence on October 6th, 1600. Given that January 1st, 1600, was a Saturday, use congruences to determine the day of the week on which the opera was first performed.\n\n*Hint: The year 1600 was a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and divisible by $400.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1600, and October 1st, 1600. \n\n- There were $5$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, and August).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and October 6th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to October 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(5)+30(3)+29(1)}}+5.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,29≡1,\\,5≡5,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5(31)+3(30)+1(29)+5 \\\\  & ≡5(3)+3(2)+(1)+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡15+6+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1+6+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡13\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date 6 days after January 1st, 1600, namely Friday." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-34ae9d51-f1ea-544e-a179-f5f86690c686"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34ae9d51-f1ea-544e-a179-f5f86690c686" :answer-field/id #uuid "34ae9d51-f1ea-544e-a179-f5f86690c686" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f7a5232b-17c7-57b3-9855-7a1be167543c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f7a5232b-17c7-57b3-9855-7a1be167543c" "answer-625f2cc4-7141-5b58-891f-19fd378aa8fa" "answer-359bf237-b12e-5567-ab3b-fdf8b157a4f4" "answer-f85803f7-5e70-5e5a-9e50-5864a6a6194a" "answer-1cf2b520-553b-53a7-88f5-8915ae76af14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7a5232b-17c7-57b3-9855-7a1be167543c" :answer/id #uuid "f7a5232b-17c7-57b3-9855-7a1be167543c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Friday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-625f2cc4-7141-5b58-891f-19fd378aa8fa" :answer/id #uuid "625f2cc4-7141-5b58-891f-19fd378aa8fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Monday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-359bf237-b12e-5567-ab3b-fdf8b157a4f4" :answer/id #uuid "359bf237-b12e-5567-ab3b-fdf8b157a4f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Saturday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f85803f7-5e70-5e5a-9e50-5864a6a6194a" :answer/id #uuid "f85803f7-5e70-5e5a-9e50-5864a6a6194a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Sunday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cf2b520-553b-53a7-88f5-8915ae76af14" :answer/id #uuid "1cf2b520-553b-53a7-88f5-8915ae76af14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Wednesday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f453d5f9-efe9-5d81-aae2-3ab414bd5f85" :question/id #uuid "f453d5f9-efe9-5d81-aae2-3ab414bd5f85" :question/math-academy-id "q-213293" :question/problem "Christmas in 2100 will be celebrated, as usual, on December 25th. Given that January 1st, 2100, is a Friday, use congruences to determine the day of the week on which Christmas will be celebrated in 2100.\n\n*Hint: The year 2100 is not a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and is not divisible by $400.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 2100, and December 1st, 2100. \n\n- There are $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There are $4$ months with $30$ days (April, June, September, and November).\n\n- There is $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and December 25th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to December 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(4)+28(1)}}+24.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,24≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+4(30)+1(28)+24 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+4(2)+(0)+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+8+0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+1+0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $1$ day after January 1st, 2100, namely Saturday." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6264c938-98d7-59e0-8745-3f11336fceb7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6264c938-98d7-59e0-8745-3f11336fceb7" :answer-field/id #uuid "6264c938-98d7-59e0-8745-3f11336fceb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0435df86-3781-513b-a46f-906712e96176" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6337ab72-5ce7-599b-9621-a448e14bf720" "answer-0435df86-3781-513b-a46f-906712e96176" "answer-69b30bc0-14ba-54bc-a56c-7790688ad638" "answer-ef77d4b9-b779-526e-a114-59633d5da258" "answer-4e6d9cbb-8c21-5758-abb5-f5e2a3269156"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6337ab72-5ce7-599b-9621-a448e14bf720" :answer/id #uuid "6337ab72-5ce7-599b-9621-a448e14bf720" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Monday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0435df86-3781-513b-a46f-906712e96176" :answer/id #uuid "0435df86-3781-513b-a46f-906712e96176" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Saturday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69b30bc0-14ba-54bc-a56c-7790688ad638" :answer/id #uuid "69b30bc0-14ba-54bc-a56c-7790688ad638" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Sunday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef77d4b9-b779-526e-a114-59633d5da258" :answer/id #uuid "ef77d4b9-b779-526e-a114-59633d5da258" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Thursday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e6d9cbb-8c21-5758-abb5-f5e2a3269156" :answer/id #uuid "4e6d9cbb-8c21-5758-abb5-f5e2a3269156" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Tuesday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152481 :question/problem "You're given that January 1st, 2000, was a Saturday. Use congruences to determine the day of the week on November 4th, 2000.\n\n*Hint: The year 2000 was a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and divisible by $400.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 2000, and November 1st, 2000. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 4th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to November 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(3)+29(1)}}+3.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,29≡1,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(29)+3 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(1)+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡14\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as January 1st, 2000, namely Saturday." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-67fd7e7a-5d04-51e0-a66a-8f26c30b7b35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-67fd7e7a-5d04-51e0-a66a-8f26c30b7b35" :answer-field/id #uuid "67fd7e7a-5d04-51e0-a66a-8f26c30b7b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c77205d4-aa6d-56d6-a0da-855fbd3a4628" :answer-field/choices ["answer-de4dc52c-f7a5-523c-96ba-60dae695e897" "answer-c77205d4-aa6d-56d6-a0da-855fbd3a4628" "answer-2695a325-110d-50bc-8dcd-637ddf9e0b70" "answer-29c90762-ad00-51c7-858e-4662c5bf0620" "answer-6ad2aa1e-2756-588f-b7c1-9126054efb93"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de4dc52c-f7a5-523c-96ba-60dae695e897" :answer/id #uuid "de4dc52c-f7a5-523c-96ba-60dae695e897" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Monday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c77205d4-aa6d-56d6-a0da-855fbd3a4628" :answer/id #uuid "c77205d4-aa6d-56d6-a0da-855fbd3a4628" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Saturday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2695a325-110d-50bc-8dcd-637ddf9e0b70" :answer/id #uuid "2695a325-110d-50bc-8dcd-637ddf9e0b70" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Sunday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29c90762-ad00-51c7-858e-4662c5bf0620" :answer/id #uuid "29c90762-ad00-51c7-858e-4662c5bf0620" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Tuesday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ad2aa1e-2756-588f-b7c1-9126054efb93" :answer/id #uuid "6ad2aa1e-2756-588f-b7c1-9126054efb93" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Wednesday" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-83693413-601d-53f0-b546-e5ed9a2ee614" :question/id #uuid "83693413-601d-53f0-b546-e5ed9a2ee614" :question/math-academy-id "q-213292" :question/problem "On November 16th, 1700, Spain's King Charles II died. Given that January 1st, 1700, was a Friday, use congruences to determine on which day of the week Charles II died.\n\n*Hint: The year 1700 was not a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and is not divisible by $400.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1700, and November 1st, 1700. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 16th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to November 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(3)+28(1)}}+15.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,15≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(28)+15 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(0)+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $4$ days after January 1st, 1700, namely Tuesday." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3d016934-cf4f-5b52-99e0-61cacbf8d0d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fie