text{mod}\\,11),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" :question/worked-solution "The subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-c≡b-c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can subtract the same integer from both sides of a congruence, keeping the modulus unchanged:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-6≡b-6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5960e740-e4ff-50fc-834d-d286a215e9e8" "answer-field-87dd9b76-f2d3-5bb2-a6a2-b11f54305d85"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5960e740-e4ff-50fc-834d-d286a215e9e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "5960e740-e4ff-50fc-834d-d286a215e9e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8e3d5d71-81ed-5e64-8e0a-e9f5fc2e9beb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5fcfef59-c316-50fe-8c86-49260a987176" "answer-8e3d5d71-81ed-5e64-8e0a-e9f5fc2e9beb" "answer-f0744143-e981-51aa-99d4-dc2a7487c678"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fcfef59-c316-50fe-8c86-49260a987176" :answer/id #uuid "5fcfef59-c316-50fe-8c86-49260a987176" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e3d5d71-81ed-5e64-8e0a-e9f5fc2e9beb" :answer/id #uuid "8e3d5d71-81ed-5e64-8e0a-e9f5fc2e9beb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0744143-e981-51aa-99d4-dc2a7487c678" :answer/id #uuid "f0744143-e981-51aa-99d4-dc2a7487c678" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-87dd9b76-f2d3-5bb2-a6a2-b11f54305d85" :answer-field/id #uuid "87dd9b76-f2d3-5bb2-a6a2-b11f54305d85" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-48634c79-74f1-5a49-99c5-f9f91903efb6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38085e10-97fd-552d-952f-870d58f7f01e" "answer-48634c79-74f1-5a49-99c5-f9f91903efb6" "answer-c0ae7b97-0af7-552c-a63e-4f9590807541"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38085e10-97fd-552d-952f-870d58f7f01e" :answer/id #uuid "38085e10-97fd-552d-952f-870d58f7f01e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48634c79-74f1-5a49-99c5-f9f91903efb6" :answer/id #uuid "48634c79-74f1-5a49-99c5-f9f91903efb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0ae7b97-0af7-552c-a63e-4f9590807541" :answer/id #uuid "c0ae7b97-0af7-552c-a63e-4f9590807541" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152482 :question/problem "Given that $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" :question/worked-solution "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nThis gives rise to the following facts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,⟺\\,a≡b+kn\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ a≡b\\,⟺\\,a+kn≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can add a multiple of the modulus $13$ to one side of the congruence. Notice that \n\n$$\n-26=(-2)\\cdot 13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-26≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8cd1fe34-1104-5712-89af-0f4b1cbab870"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8cd1fe34-1104-5712-89af-0f4b1cbab870" :answer-field/id #uuid "8cd1fe34-1104-5712-89af-0f4b1cbab870" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-9871872a-ffa5-55e4-84a9-0f801d56b2c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18713113-2b94-57e2-8357-e9ce34b2ae30" "answer-1d657fb4-9478-55fe-b9df-f986e75a7456" "answer-9871872a-ffa5-55e4-84a9-0f801d56b2c5" "answer-2502acb8-ea55-50ee-af02-180727671cde" "answer-3a3727e0-913c-55e1-a4c8-ee04973b5961"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18713113-2b94-57e2-8357-e9ce34b2ae30" :answer/id #uuid "18713113-2b94-57e2-8357-e9ce34b2ae30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d657fb4-9478-55fe-b9df-f986e75a7456" :answer/id #uuid "1d657fb4-9478-55fe-b9df-f986e75a7456" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9871872a-ffa5-55e4-84a9-0f801d56b2c5" :answer/id #uuid "9871872a-ffa5-55e4-84a9-0f801d56b2c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "26" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2502acb8-ea55-50ee-af02-180727671cde" :answer/id #uuid "2502acb8-ea55-50ee-af02-180727671cde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a3727e0-913c-55e1-a4c8-ee04973b5961" :answer/id #uuid "3a3727e0-913c-55e1-a4c8-ee04973b5961" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5f92504f-2a88-577a-b7ee-007131f73594" :question/id #uuid "5f92504f-2a88-577a-b7ee-007131f73594" :question/math-academy-id "q-212951" :question/problem "Given that $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" :question/worked-solution "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nThis gives rise to the following facts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,⟺\\,a≡b+kn\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ a≡b\\,⟺\\,a+kn≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can add a multiple of the modulus $15$ to one side of the congruence. Notice that \n\n$$\n-45=(-3)\\cdot 15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-45≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f75f83ea-8131-580b-8fa9-3a2ff784fb28"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f75f83ea-8131-580b-8fa9-3a2ff784fb28" :answer-field/id #uuid "f75f83ea-8131-580b-8fa9-3a2ff784fb28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-9ffbd37e-9a23-5f00-b373-e65f94d2e33d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-680ce3bf-ee06-5817-91b5-bdfc17943e16" "answer-82caf35d-0e19-5485-aae6-652a04bac176" "answer-9ffbd37e-9a23-5f00-b373-e65f94d2e33d" "answer-e201c92d-f1aa-50d9-8ac8-da22772174db" "answer-0ea93020-b73c-51c8-92b7-9b07d3bc82c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-680ce3bf-ee06-5817-91b5-bdfc17943e16" :answer/id #uuid "680ce3bf-ee06-5817-91b5-bdfc17943e16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82caf35d-0e19-5485-aae6-652a04bac176" :answer/id #uuid "82caf35d-0e19-5485-aae6-652a04bac176" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ffbd37e-9a23-5f00-b373-e65f94d2e33d" :answer/id #uuid "9ffbd37e-9a23-5f00-b373-e65f94d2e33d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e201c92d-f1aa-50d9-8ac8-da22772174db" :answer/id #uuid "e201c92d-f1aa-50d9-8ac8-da22772174db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ea93020-b73c-51c8-92b7-9b07d3bc82c8" :answer/id #uuid "0ea93020-b73c-51c8-92b7-9b07d3bc82c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153446 :tutorial/content "Recall that two integers $a$ and $b$ are *congruent modulo* $n$ if they have the same remainder when divided by $n.$\n\nMoreover, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $a-b.$\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a-b = kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in\\mathbb Z.$ \n\nA convenient property of congruences is that we can add and subtract on both sides, just as with regular equations. \n\nConsider, for instance, the following true statement:\n\n$$\n22\\equiv 10 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nThe **addition property** allows us to add an integer to both sides. For example, adding $2$ to both sides gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22+2 & ≡10+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 24 & ≡12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is also a true statement since $24$ and $12$ both have a remainder of zero when divided by $12.$\n\nSimilarly, the **subtraction property** allows us to subtract an integer from both sides. For example, subtracting $9$ from both sides of our original statement gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22-9 & ≡10-9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 13 & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $13$ and $1$ both have a remainder of $1$ when divided by $12.$\n\nIn general, the addition and subtraction properties state the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,⟺\\,a+c≡b+c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ a≡b\\,⟺\\,a-c≡b-c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153447 :tutorial/content "According to the definition of modular congruence, we have that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)  \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = b+ kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Substituting $b=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid a  \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = kn\n$$\n\nThis gives rise to the following important fact:\n\n*Any integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $n.$*\n\nFor example, the numbers $10,$ $-25,$ and $75$ are all are all multiples of ${\\color{blue}5},$ so they are all congruent to $0$ modulo ${\\color{blue}5}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ -25 & =(-5)\\cdot 5≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 75 & =15\\cdot 5≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153450 :tutorial/content "Let's try to simplify the right-hand side of the following congruence:\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 102 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\n$$\n\nBy \"simplify,\" we mean that we wish to transform the number on the right-hand side to be the smallest possible positive integer. In other words, we want to write\n\n$$\nx \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\\qquad\\textrm{for}\\qquad 0 \\leq r < 5.\n$$\n\nWhen we add $0$ to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing $0$ as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to $0\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:{\\color{black}n}).$ \n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  ${\\color{blue}5} \\cdot (-20) \\equiv 0 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}5})$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡102\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x+0 & ≡102+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x & \\text{ }≡102+5\\cdot (-20)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x & ≡102-100\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x \\equiv 102\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$ is equivalent to $x \\equiv 2\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,5).$\n\nOur answer is correct because $102$ and $2$ both have a remainder of $2$ when divided by $5,$ and $0 \\leq  2 < 5.$\n\n**Note:** By the division algorithm, there is exactly one value of $r$ that satisfies\n\n$$\nx \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\\qquad\\textrm{for}\\qquad 0 \\leq r < n.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153472 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we've been working with the so-called addition property of modular arithmetic.\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+c\\equiv b+c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the addition property by writing $a\\equiv b$ in terms of divisibility:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\, & ⟺\\,a-b=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a+c)-(b+c)=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a+c)≡(b+c)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe subtraction property is proved similarly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\, & ⟺\\,a-b=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a-c)-(b-c)=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a-c)≡(b-c)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152340 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
